course: medicine
lesson: 68
title: The coach who shouts and parents on the touchline
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When Lily's coach in Birmingham roars «MOVE, LILY! WHAT ARE YOU DOING?», her brain's alarm, the amygdala, fires: fight, flight or freeze, blood rushes to the big muscles, and thinking and fine footwork get worse, so she freezes with the ball at her feet.
explain: Ethan in Chicago hears «Shoot!» from his mom, «Pass!» from his coach and «Here!» from three teammates all at once; the brain can only follow one instruction at a time, it hesitates for a split second, and the chance is gone.
explain: Loud isn't the same as nasty: Dylan's coach in Manchester shouts «Man on!» and «Turn!» all match and helps his players, but Maddie's coach in Glasgow calls her «a useless wee numpty» («numpty» is Scottish for an idiot) every week, and that is bullying.
explain: Many grassroots referees are teenagers learning the job, and touchline abuse is a big reason they quit, so «Ref, you're blind!» from a 42-year-old dad at a 15-year-old helps nobody: no ref, no game.
explain: Some leagues try «silent touchline» weekends where adults may only clap, and many kids say they play more freely; parents cheer, the coach coaches, and the best words after a game are «I love watching you play».
fact: Fear sets off the brain's alarm system, the amygdala, which prepares the body to fight, run or freeze.
fact: In a fear response blood goes to the big muscles, and careful thinking and fine skills such as close ball control get worse.
fact: Being screamed at usually makes children's skills and decisions worse, not better.
fact: The brain struggles to follow several shouted instructions at once in the middle of a move.
fact: Short, loud information calls such as «Man on!» or «Time!» help players; insults and anger hurt them.
fact: Many grassroots referees are teenagers, and abuse from the touchline is a big reason referees quit.
fact: At grassroots level one referee watches the whole pitch with no video help, so some calls will be wrong, for both teams, over a season.
fact: Some grassroots leagues hold silent-support weekends where adults may only clap.
fact: Straight after a match children are tired and emotional, so a list of mistakes in the car lands much harder.
fact: Being put down by a coach week after week is bullying; clubs have a welfare officer to tell, and a parent can help.
fact: Dreading something can make the body release the stress hormone cortisol beforehand, and a sore tummy is a common sign of that worry.
fact: If adults fight on the touchline, a child should go to the coach or a safe adult, not try to stop it.
fact: Children copy the adults around them, so a coach who shouts insults can turn players into shouters too.
fact: «Muppet» is British slang for a silly person; «arvo» is Australian for afternoon; «chill now now» in South Africa means calm down soon, in a minute.
exercise: Lily freezes when her coach roars at her. What has happened? | Her brain's alarm set off fight, flight or freeze | She has forgotten the rules of football | Her boots have stuck to the mud | She is too tired to move her legs
exercise: In a fear response, what happens to fine footwork? | It gets worse | It gets much better | It stays the same | It turns automatic
exercise: Does screaming at kids make them play better? | No, fear usually makes skills worse | Yes, it works every time, without fail | Yes, as long as it's loud enough to hear | Only when they're losing by a lot of goals
exercise: Ethan hears five voices yelling at once and loses the ball. Why? | The brain can follow only one instruction at a time | He couldn't hear any of them clearly | His mom gave the wrong advice | Too many voices make the ball slippery
exercise: Who should give instructions during the game? | The coach; parents cheer | Every parent on the touchline | Whoever shouts loudest | The other team's parents
exercise: Dylan's coach shouts «Man on!» all match but never a nasty word. Is that bad coaching? | No, loud information helps players | Yes, all shouting at players is bad coaching | Yes, good coaches must whisper their calls | No, but only if his team ends up winning
exercise: Kian's dad yells «Ref, you're blind!» at a 15-year-old referee. What does abuse like this often cause? | Young refs quitting the game | Better decisions next time | A fairer match for his son | The ref playing for his team
exercise: How many refs watch the 22 players at grassroots? | Usually one | Usually five | Usually none | Usually twenty-two
exercise: What do many kids say about silent touchline weekends? | They play more freely | They hate the quiet and stop trying | They score fewer goals | They miss being shouted at
exercise: Rohan's mum shouts every time he touches the ball. How does he feel? | Embarrassed, and tense players make more mistakes | Proud, and he plays perfectly | Relaxed, he loves the noise | Angry at the referee
exercise: Sophie's dad lists her mistakes in the car. What would be better? | «I love watching you play» | A longer list of mistakes | A list written on paper | Talking only about the referee
exercise: Maddie's coach calls her a «useless wee numpty» every week. What should she do? | Tell a parent or the club welfare officer | Toughen up, that's just coaching | Shout it back at the coach | Quit sport for good
exercise: Why does Maddie get a sore tummy before training? | Dread releases stress hormones beforehand | She eats her tea far too fast before training | The pitch is too cold for tummies | Her boots are too tight
exercise: Two dads square up on the touchline. What should a child do? | Go to the coach or a safe adult | Run in and try to pull them apart | Join in to stick up for their own dad | Keep playing the game and ignore it
exercise: Callum yells «Sort it out, ya muppet!» at teammates, like his coach. What do leaders do instead? | Encourage and give clear calls | Shout the loudest insults | Copy the coach's angriest words | Stay silent the whole match
exercise: Two dads stop a 60-minute game at 35 minutes. How many minutes are lost? | 25 | 35 | 60 | 95
test: Coach: «I scream so they try harder, works every time». What does the science say? | Fear usually makes skills worse | Screaming improves fine footwork | Kids' brains need loud alarms | It works only for goalkeepers
test: «ref's against us, every call goes their way, swear down». What's more likely? | One ref, a hard angle; mistakes go both ways | The ref was paid by the other team before the game | Refs always favour the away team | The ref can't see anything at all
test: Ava from Kent: «silent touchline was proper weird, but I liked it». Why might she have played better? | She could think for herself without shouts | The other team couldn't hear the ref | The grass was quieter so the ball rolled | Silence makes legs run faster and kick harder
test: What would happen to grassroots football if all teenage refs quit because of abuse? | There'd be no refs and fewer games | Parents would happily ref every game better instead | Kids would never argue again | Games would go on just the same
test: Amara: «shame, coach, chill now now» as he screams at the ref. What is she telling him? | Calm down, soon | Shout louder now | Leave the pitch | Well done, coach
test: Sophie stares out of the window in the arvo while her dad lists mistakes. Why does it land so hard? | She's tired and emotional after the game | Afternoon light makes ears more sensitive to words | Cars make criticism louder | She didn't play in the match
test: «my coach calls me thick every week, that's just footy innit». Right? | No, that's bullying; tell the welfare officer | Yes, all coaches have to do it | Yes, it toughens players up | No, but only if he swears
test: In a film, the screaming coach turns a team of losers into champions in a week. What's more true? | Calm, clear coaching usually works better | Screaming always makes champions in the end | Shouting makes kids run faster | Teams win because coaches yell
test: Ethan's mom: «I'll just shout louder so he hears me over the coach». What would happen? | Even more confusion; parents should just cheer | He'd follow her and score every time | The coach would stop talking | Ethan would hear only her voice
test: Callum's teammates start making more mistakes after he yells at them. Why? | Their confidence drops and nerves cause errors | They are trying to annoy him back by playing badly | Yelling makes the ball heavier | They can't hear the referee
test: Lily's heart goes from 120 to 160 beats a minute when the coach roars. How many more beats a minute? | 40 | 280 | 160 | 30
exam: Identify the region of the brain principally responsible for the fear response observed in a child subjected to shouting. | The amygdala | The cerebellum | The spinal cord | The eardrum
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of fear upon the performance of fine motor skills? | Such skills are typically impaired | Such skills are invariably enhanced | Such skills are wholly unaffected | Such skills are transferred to the coach
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning abuse directed at grassroots referees. | It is a principal cause of referees leaving the game | It reliably improves the accuracy of decisions | It is required by the laws of the game | It is directed exclusively at adult referees
exam: It is given that a child is subjected to repeated insults by a coach. State the appropriate course of action. | The matter is to be reported to a parent or the club welfare officer | The child is to accept such treatment as customary | The child is to reciprocate the insults | The child is to withdraw from all physical activity
